 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title       = 'Are Ravens Dangerous? What You Need to Know';
const description = 'Ravens are not generally dangerous to humans. This page examines documented cases of raven aggression, what actually poses risk, and how to respond if a raven acts aggressively near you.';
const path        = '/questions/are-ravens-dangerous/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'Are Ravens Dangerous?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Can a raven hurt a human?', a: 'A raven is physically capable of causing minor injuries — their bill can cut or scratch, and their talons are sharp. However, serious injury from ravens to humans is extremely rare. The great majority of reported raven-human "attacks" are nest-defence swooping during the breeding season, which involves brief contact or near-miss passes rather than sustained attacks. A raven weighs 700–1,600 g — large for a bird but not physically capable of causing serious harm to a healthy adult human.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens a danger to pets or livestock?', a: 'Ravens are occasionally recorded preying on very small animals. Well-documented impacts include: ravens preying on eggs and chicks of ground-nesting birds (a conservation concern for some species); occasional predation on very young or sick lambs (documented but contested in terms of scale); and displacement of smaller birds from food sources through dominance. Healthy adult livestock are not at risk from ravens. The scale of lamb predation by ravens is disputed — some accounts are well-documented; others may confuse predation with scavenging of lambs that died from other causes.' },
  { q: 'Should I be scared of ravens?', a: 'No. Ravens are intelligent, curious wild birds that generally avoid humans. Outside of nest-defence context (February–June near active nests), wild ravens almost never approach humans aggressively. The cultural association of ravens with omens and death has created an exaggerated perception of danger. Ravens that become habituated to humans at campsites or tourist areas may be bold and persistent about food, which can be annoying but is not dangerous. Respect their space near nest sites during the breeding season, and you are extremely unlikely to have any negative interaction with a wild raven.' },
];
---

