  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Question · Biology</p>
    <h1>Are Ravens Dangerous? What You Need to Know</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens are <strong>not generally dangerous to humans</strong>. Wild ravens avoid people in most situations. The main exception is nest-defence behaviour during the breeding season (February–June), when nesting pairs may swoop at people who approach the nest. Serious injury from raven attacks is extremely rare. Ravens weigh 700–1,600 g and cannot cause serious physical harm to a healthy adult.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Physical Capability</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> To assess danger, it helps to understand raven physical capability:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Weight:</strong> Common Ravens weigh 700–1,600 g — large for a passerine but small compared to raptors capable of injuring humans (golden eagles, great horned owls).</li>
      <li><strong>Bill:</strong> powerful and arched, capable of cutting through the hide of large mammal carcasses. A direct peck or bite can cause cuts, bruising, or punctures — painful but not life-threatening.</li>
      <li><strong>Talons:</strong> not raptorial; ravens use their feet for balance and manipulation, not for gripping and puncturing prey as hawks do. Talon-caused injuries are uncommon in raven attacks.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Circumstances of Raven Aggression</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven aggression toward humans is almost always context-specific:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nest defence:</strong> the most common context. Pairs with active nests will swoop at anything — humans, dogs, foxes — that approaches within 50–150 m of the nest. Intensity peaks around hatching and early chick-rearing.</li>
      <li><strong>Food competition:</strong> habituated ravens at campsites or picnic areas may attempt to steal food and can bite fingers if resisted. This is bold behaviour, not predatory behaviour.</li>
      <li><strong>Handling:</strong> ravens that are caught, cornered, or handled will bite in self-defence. Banders and researchers wear gloves. This is not relevant to encounters with wild, free ravens.</li>
      <li><strong>Captive/imprinted birds:</strong> ravens raised by humans can be aggressive to their carers in social testing behaviour. Entirely different from wild-bird encounters.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Ravens and Livestock</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens have been documented occasionally preying on newborn and very young lambs (particularly stillborn or weak individuals), young rabbits, and eggs and chicks of ground-nesting birds. The extent of lamb predation is contested — some accounts in pastoral areas of Scotland, Scandinavia, and western North America document genuine predation; others reflect scavenging of lambs that died from other causes. Adult livestock are not at risk from ravens.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
